dahite","H241":"ear","H5971":"people","H2346":"wall","H428":"these","H3427":"to dwell","H398":"to eat","H2716":"dung","H6675":"filth","H8354":"to drink","H7890":"urine","H4325":"water","H7272":"foot","H5973":"with","H7121":"call to","H6963":"voice","H5377":"to deceive","H5337":"to rescue","H3027":"hand","H6213":"do","H1293":"blessing","H1612":"vine","H8384":"fig","H953":"pit","H5704":"till","H3947":"take","H1715":"grain","H8492":"new wine","H3899":"food","H3754":"vineyard","H6435":"lest","H5496":"to incite","H1471":"nation","H346":"where?","H2574":"Hamath","H774":"Arpad","H5617":"Sepharvaim","H8111":"Samaria","H2790":"to plow/plot","H6030":"to dwell","H4687":"commandment","H7167":"to tear","H899":"treachery","H5046":"to tell"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","And. These four chapters are taken from 4 Kings xviii., &c., as a sort of explanation of what Isaias has been foretelling. (Calmet) — An abridgment also occurs, 2 Paralipomenon xxxii."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Altar. Such is the blindness of infidels, that they confound what is done to destroy idols, with their worship. (Worthington)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[12,null,0,"Homilies on Matthew","Homily 37",0,407,"… For as filth and mud close up the ears of our flesh, so do the harlot's songs, and worldly news, and debts, and the business of usury and loans, close up the ear of the mind, worse than any filth; nay rather, they do not close it up only, but also make it unclean. And they are putting dung in your ears, who tell you of these things. And that which the barbarian threatened, saying, \"Ye shall eat your own dung,\" and what follows; this do these men also make you undergo, not in word, but in deeds; or rather, somewhat even much worse. …"]],"fau":[["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 10 (1888)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf110"]]}